  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the full life cycle of an offshore wind project, showing how coastal industrial infrastructure supports development from planning through operation. In the pre-construction stage, a survey vessel maps and characterizes the seabed and wind resource, while ports, vessels, and transmission links are developed onshore. The image emphasizes the need for heavy port infrastructure, including a high-capacity ring crane, manufacturing buildings, and grid upgrades, as well as a broader supply chain of component and subcomponent suppliers.\n\nIn the construction stage, the same port acts as an assembly and staging hub for turbine parts such as blades, towers, nacelles, cables, and substructures. Installation vessels and cable-laying ships place turbines offshore, connect them with array cables, and link them through a floating offshore substation and export cables to a land-based substation and the main power grid.\n\nIn the operation phase, the wind farm is complete and attention shifts to maintenance. Service and maintenance vessels continue traveling between the port and turbines, while the port supports repair work. Overall, the figure highlights that offshore wind depends not just on turbines at sea, but on an integrated coastal manufacturing, logistics, and electrical network.",
